{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016QPQN4M3DkmTs3oxcTZkuK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texport interface BlogPost {\n2\t  slug: string;\n3\t  title: string;\n4\t  excerpt: string;\n5\t  content: string; // HTML string\n6\t  date: string; // YYYY-MM-DD\n7\t  category: string;\n8\t  author: string;\n9\t  readTime: string;\n10\t  readingTimeMinutes: number;\n11\t  tags: string[];\n12\t  featuredImage: string;\n13\t}\n14\t\n15\t/** Compute reading time from HTML content (200 wpm, minimum 1 min). */\n16\tfunction computeReadingTime(html: string): number {\n17\t  const text = html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, \" \").replace(/\\s+/g, \" \").trim();\n18\t  const wordCount = text.split(\" \").filter(Boolean).length;\n19\t  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(wordCount / 200));\n20\t}\n21\t\n22\t/** Converts a category label to a URL-safe slug. e.g. \"Food & Drink\" → \"food-drink\" */\n23\texport function categoryToSlug(category: string): string {\n24\t  return category\n25\t    .toLowerCase()\n26\t    .replace(/[^a-z0-9\\s]/g, \"\")\n27\t    .trim()\n28\t    .replace(/\\s+/g, \"-\");\n29\t}\n30\t\n31\t/** Converts a category slug back to a display label, or returns null if not found. */\n32\texport function slugToCategory(slug: string): string | null {\n33\t  const found = blogPosts.find((p) => categoryToSlug(p.category) === slug);\n34\t  return found ? found.category : null;\n35\t}\n36\t\n37\t/** All unique categories in the blog. */\n38\texport function getUniqueCategories(): string[] {\n39\t  return [...new Set(blogPosts.map((p) => p.category))];\n40\t}\n41\t\n42\tconst rawPosts: Omit<BlogPost, \"readingTimeMinutes\">[] = [\n43\t  {\n44\t    slug: \"ultimate-jb-food-guide-backpackers\",\n45\t    title: \"The Ultimate Johor Bahru Food Guide for Backpackers (2026)\",\n46\t    excerpt:\n47\t      \"From RM5 hawker meals to legendary seafood spots — everything a budget traveller needs to eat their way through JB like a local.\",\n48\t    date: \"2026-02-20\",\n49\t    category: \"Food & Drink\",\n50\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n51\t    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n52\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"food\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"malaysia\", \"hawker\"],\n53\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-food-guide.jpg\",\n54\t    content: `\n55\t<h2>Why JB Is a Backpacker's Food Paradise</h2>\n56\t<p>Johor Bahru sits at the crossroads of Malay, Chinese, Indian, and Peranakan culinary traditions — and the prices are absurdly low. A full meal at a hawker centre runs RM5–12, a pulled tea costs RM2, and the quality of food rivals cities ten times its size. For backpackers crossing the border from Singapore, where a similar meal costs SGD8–15, JB feels like a food revelation.</p>\n57\t\n58\t<p>The city doesn't have Michelin stars or Instagram-bait restaurants. What it has is better: generations-old recipes, family-run stalls, and an eating culture where quality is the only way to survive. If you're staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel in Taman Pelangi</a>, you're in one of the best food neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n59\t\n60\t<h2>Breakfast: The Malaysian Way</h2>\n61\t<p><strong>Roti canai</strong> is the quintessential Malaysian breakfast — flaky, buttery flatbread torn by hand and dipped into dhal or fish curry. Every mamak (Indian-Muslim coffee shop) makes it, and it costs RM1.50–3. Pair it with a <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled milk tea, RM2) and you've got a breakfast that costs less than a Singapore MRT ride.</p>\n62\t\n63\t<p>If you want something heartier, try <strong>nasi lemak</strong> — coconut rice served with sambal chilli, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber, and a hard-boiled egg. The stall versions, wrapped in banana leaf, run RM3–5 and are available from dawn. For the adventurous, <strong>nasi kerabu</strong> (blue-coloured herbed rice) appears at Malay stalls and is worth seeking out.</p>\n64\t\n65\t<h2>Lunch: Hawker Centre Essentials</h2>\n66\t<p>JB's hawker centres are the backbone of the city's food culture. At lunch, every centre buzzes with office workers, families, and the occasional bewildered tourist who can't believe the prices. Head to <strong>Sik Bao Sin</strong> near Taman Pelangi for mixed rice — point at whatever looks good behind the glass, and the uncle will pile your plate for RM5–8.</p>\n67\t\n68\t<p>For noodle lovers, <strong>mee rebus</strong> is a Johorean specialty: yellow noodles in a thick, slightly sweet potato-based gravy, topped with boiled egg, tofu, and fried shallots. It's hearty, warming, and unlike anything you'll find elsewhere in Malaysia. <strong>Laksa Johor</strong> is even more distinctive — it uses spaghetti-like noodles instead of rice noodles, swimming in a rich fish-based curry. It's divisive, but locals are fiercely proud of it.</p>\n69\t\n70\t<p>Check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Travel Guide</a> for a walking map of the best hawker centres within reach of the hostel.</p>\n71\t\n72\t<h2>Dinner: Seafood and Night Markets</h2>\n73\t<p><strong>San Low Seafood</strong>, a five-minute walk from Pelangi Capsule, is legendary among locals for its <strong>San Lou Bee Hoon</strong> — dry-fried vermicelli with prawns, egg, and a savoury soy glaze. The dish looks simple but the wok technique is masterful. Budget RM15–25 per person for a seafood dinner here, which is still a fraction of Singapore prices.</p>\n74\t\n75\t<p>If you prefer street food, JB's night markets (pasar malam) are unmissable. <strong>Taman Sentosa Night Market</strong> runs Thursday and Sunday nights and stretches for hundreds of metres: satay sticks (RM0.60 each), lok-lok (skewered meats dipped in boiling broth, RM1–2 per stick), martabak (stuffed pancakes), and fresh coconut water. Budget RM15–25 for a full night market feast.</p>\n76\t\n77\t<h2>Snacks and Drinks</h2>\n78\t<p><strong>Otak-otak</strong> — spiced fish paste wrapped in banana leaf and grilled over charcoal — is sold by roadside vendors for RM1–2 per piece. Buy five and eat them walking. <strong>Kuih</strong> (bite-sized Malay cakes and sweets) appear at morning markets and cost RM0.50–1 each. Try <strong>kuih lapis</strong> (rainbow layered cake) and <strong>ondeh-ondeh</strong> (pandan balls filled with palm sugar).</p>\n79\t\n80\t<p>For drinks, the <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled tea) is an art form. Watch the vendor pour boiling tea between two cups from arm's length, creating a frothy, perfectly mixed drink. <strong>Cham</strong> (coffee mixed with tea) is another local favourite. Fresh sugarcane juice costs RM3 and is the best remedy for JB's tropical heat.</p>\n81\t\n82\t<h2>Budget Breakdown</h2>\n83\t<p>Here's what a day of eating in JB actually costs:</p>\n84\t<ul>\n85\t  <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Roti canai + teh tarik — RM5</li>\n86\t  <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Hawker centre mixed rice — RM8</li>\n87\t  <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Otak-otak + sugarcane juice — RM5</li>\n88\t  <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Night market feast — RM20</li>\n89\t  <li><strong>Total:</strong> RM38 per day (approximately SGD11 or USD8)</li>\n90\t</ul>\n91\t\n92\t<p>That's three full meals, snacks, and drinks for less than the cost of one restaurant meal in Singapore. This is why backpackers who discover JB's food scene never want to leave.</p>\n93\t\n94\t<p>Ready to eat your way through JB? <a href=\"/book\">Book a capsule at Pelangi</a> and start your food adventure in one of the best eating neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n95\t`,\n96\t  },\n97\t  {\n98\t    slug: \"singapore-to-jb-day-trip-causeway-guide\",\n99\t    title: \"Singapore to JB Day Trip Guide: How to Cross the Causeway (2026)\",\n100\t    excerpt:\n101\t      \"The complete step-by-step guide to crossing the Causeway from Singapore to Johor Bahru — transport options, immigration tips, costs, and what to do once you arrive.\",\n102\t    date: \"2026-02-25\",\n103\t    category: \"Travel Tips\",\n104\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n105\t    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n106\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"causeway\", \"border-crossing\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\"],\n107\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/causeway-guide.jpg\",\n108\t    content: `\n109\t<h2>Why Cross the Causeway?</h2>\n110\t<p>The Johor–Singapore Causeway is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world, connecting Singapore's Woodlands district to Johor Bahru, Malaysia. For travellers based in Singapore, JB offers dramatically cheaper food, shopping, and accommodation — making it the most popular day trip from the island. For backpackers, it's even more strategic: stay in JB at a fraction of Singapore prices and day-trip into Singapore whenever you want.</p>\n111\t\n112\t<p>The crossing itself is straightforward once you know the drill. This guide covers every option, common mistakes, and what to expect in 2026.</p>\n113\t\n114\t<h2>Option 1: Bus from Woodlands (Most Popular)</h2>\n115\t<p>The <strong>Causeway Link CW1</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit 170</strong> buses depart from Woodlands Checkpoint on the Singapore side. Here's the process:</p>\n116\t<ol>\n117\t  <li><strong>Get to Woodlands:</strong> Take the MRT to Woodlands or Kranji station. From Kranji, bus 160 goes directly to the checkpoint.</li>\n118\t  <li><strong>Clear Singapore immigration:</strong> Everyone exits the bus, walks through Singapore passport control, and re-boards.</li>\n119\t  <li><strong>Cross the Causeway:</strong> The bus drives across the 1km bridge. Takes 5 minutes without traffic.</li>\n120\t  <li><strong>Clear Malaysian immigration:</strong> Everyone exits again, clears Malaysian passport control at JB CIQ (Customs, Immigration & Quarantine).</li>\n121\t  <li><strong>Arrive in JB:</strong> You're now at JB Sentral, connected to City Square Mall and the city centre.</li>\n122\t</ol>\n123\t<p>Cost: SGD1.50–2 per way. The entire crossing takes 30–60 minutes on a good day, or 2–3 hours during peak periods (Friday evenings, public holidays, school holidays).</p>\n124\t\n125\t<h2>Option 2: Train (RTS Link — Coming Soon)</h2>\n126\t<p>The Johor Bahru–Singapore Rapid Transit System (RTS Link) has been in development and will eventually connect Bukit Chagar in JB to Woodlands North in Singapore. Until it's operational, the bus remains your best bet. Check KTMB for any shuttle train services that may be running between JB Sentral and Woodlands.</p>\n127\t\n128\t<h2>Option 3: Private Car or Grab</h2>\n129\t<p>You can take a Grab from Singapore to JB, but you'll need to exit the car at both immigration checkpoints and re-board. Some drivers won't do cross-border trips. A more reliable option is booking a private transfer — expect SGD40–60 for the car. For solo backpackers, the bus is significantly cheaper and barely slower.</p>\n130\t\n131\t<h2>Immigration Tips</h2>\n132\t<ul>\n133\t  <li><strong>Passport validity:</strong> Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months from the date of entry into Malaysia.</li>\n134\t  <li><strong>Malaysia Digital Arrival Card (MDAC):</strong> Since 2024, most nationalities must complete the MDAC online before arrival. Do this before you reach the border — it takes 5 minutes and is free.</li>\n135\t  <li><strong>Peak hours to avoid:</strong> Friday 5–9pm (Singapore to JB), Sunday 5–9pm (JB to Singapore). Weekday mornings are the smoothest.</li>\n136\t  <li><strong>Keep your departure card:</strong> Malaysian immigration staples a small card into your passport. Don't lose it — you need it when leaving Malaysia.</li>\n137\t</ul>\n138\t\n139\t<h2>What to Do Once You Arrive in JB</h2>\n140\t<p>JB CIQ drops you at <strong>JB Sentral</strong>, the city's main transport hub. From here:</p>\n141\t<ul>\n142\t  <li><strong>City Square Mall</strong> is connected directly — air-conditioned shopping, food court, and money changers.</li>\n143\t  <li><strong>Jalan Tan Hiok Nee</strong> (10 min walk) — heritage street with indie cafes, art galleries, and the best street murals in JB.</li>\n144\t  <li><strong>Taman Pelangi</strong> (10 min Grab ride, RM8) — the neighbourhood where <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> is located, surrounded by hawker centres and local restaurants.</li>\n145\t</ul>\n146\t\n147\t<p>For a complete rundown of activities, attractions, and local tips, check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Backpacker's Guide</a>.</p>\n148\t\n149\t<h2>Costs: Singapore vs JB</h2>\n150\t<p>The whole reason to cross the Causeway is the price difference:</p>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6da17fb6-3289-4255-a288-226ac7439267","uuid":"dc6692e3-c6ec-4680-a6db-5a736f7b890d","timestamp":"2026-04-14T09:32:55.071Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"export interface BlogPost {\n  slug: string;\n  title: string;\n  excerpt: string;\n  content: string; // HTML string\n  date: string; // YYYY-MM-DD\n  category: string;\n  author: string;\n  readTime: string;\n  readingTimeMinutes: number;\n  tags: string[];\n  featuredImage: string;\n}\n\n/** Compute reading time from HTML content (200 wpm, minimum 1 min). */\nfunction computeReadingTime(html: string): number {\n  const text = html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, \" \").replace(/\\s+/g, \" \").trim();\n  const wordCount = text.split(\" \").filter(Boolean).length;\n  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(wordCount / 200));\n}\n\n/** Converts a category label to a URL-safe slug. e.g. \"Food & Drink\" → \"food-drink\" */\nexport function categoryToSlug(category: string): string {\n  return category\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9\\s]/g, \"\")\n    .trim()\n    .replace(/\\s+/g, \"-\");\n}\n\n/** Converts a category slug back to a display label, or returns null if not found. */\nexport function slugToCategory(slug: string): string | null {\n  const found = blogPosts.find((p) => categoryToSlug(p.category) === slug);\n  return found ? found.category : null;\n}\n\n/** All unique categories in the blog. */\nexport function getUniqueCategories(): string[] {\n  return [...new Set(blogPosts.map((p) => p.category))];\n}\n\nconst rawPosts: Omit<BlogPost, \"readingTimeMinutes\">[] = [\n  {\n    slug: \"ultimate-jb-food-guide-backpackers\",\n    title: \"The Ultimate Johor Bahru Food Guide for Backpackers (2026)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"From RM5 hawker meals to legendary seafood spots — everything a budget traveller needs to eat their way through JB like a local.\",\n    date: \"2026-02-20\",\n    category: \"Food & Drink\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"food\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"malaysia\", \"hawker\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-food-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Why JB Is a Backpacker's Food Paradise</h2>\n<p>Johor Bahru sits at the crossroads of Malay, Chinese, Indian, and Peranakan culinary traditions — and the prices are absurdly low. A full meal at a hawker centre runs RM5–12, a pulled tea costs RM2, and the quality of food rivals cities ten times its size. For backpackers crossing the border from Singapore, where a similar meal costs SGD8–15, JB feels like a food revelation.</p>\n\n<p>The city doesn't have Michelin stars or Instagram-bait restaurants. What it has is better: generations-old recipes, family-run stalls, and an eating culture where quality is the only way to survive. If you're staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel in Taman Pelangi</a>, you're in one of the best food neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n\n<h2>Breakfast: The Malaysian Way</h2>\n<p><strong>Roti canai</strong> is the quintessential Malaysian breakfast — flaky, buttery flatbread torn by hand and dipped into dhal or fish curry. Every mamak (Indian-Muslim coffee shop) makes it, and it costs RM1.50–3. Pair it with a <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled milk tea, RM2) and you've got a breakfast that costs less than a Singapore MRT ride.</p>\n\n<p>If you want something heartier, try <strong>nasi lemak</strong> — coconut rice served with sambal chilli, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber, and a hard-boiled egg. The stall versions, wrapped in banana leaf, run RM3–5 and are available from dawn. For the adventurous, <strong>nasi kerabu</strong> (blue-coloured herbed rice) appears at Malay stalls and is worth seeking out.</p>\n\n<h2>Lunch: Hawker Centre Essentials</h2>\n<p>JB's hawker centres are the backbone of the city's food culture. At lunch, every centre buzzes with office workers, families, and the occasional bewildered tourist who can't believe the prices. Head to <strong>Sik Bao Sin</strong> near Taman Pelangi for mixed rice — point at whatever looks good behind the glass, and the uncle will pile your plate for RM5–8.</p>\n\n<p>For noodle lovers, <strong>mee rebus</strong> is a Johorean specialty: yellow noodles in a thick, slightly sweet potato-based gravy, topped with boiled egg, tofu, and fried shallots. It's hearty, warming, and unlike anything you'll find elsewhere in Malaysia. <strong>Laksa Johor</strong> is even more distinctive — it uses spaghetti-like noodles instead of rice noodles, swimming in a rich fish-based curry. It's divisive, but locals are fiercely proud of it.</p>\n\n<p>Check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Travel Guide</a> for a walking map of the best hawker centres within reach of the hostel.</p>\n\n<h2>Dinner: Seafood and Night Markets</h2>\n<p><strong>San Low Seafood</strong>, a five-minute walk from Pelangi Capsule, is legendary among locals for its <strong>San Lou Bee Hoon</strong> — dry-fried vermicelli with prawns, egg, and a savoury soy glaze. The dish looks simple but the wok technique is masterful. Budget RM15–25 per person for a seafood dinner here, which is still a fraction of Singapore prices.</p>\n\n<p>If you prefer street food, JB's night markets (pasar malam) are unmissable. <strong>Taman Sentosa Night Market</strong> runs Thursday and Sunday nights and stretches for hundreds of metres: satay sticks (RM0.60 each), lok-lok (skewered meats dipped in boiling broth, RM1–2 per stick), martabak (stuffed pancakes), and fresh coconut water. Budget RM15–25 for a full night market feast.</p>\n\n<h2>Snacks and Drinks</h2>\n<p><strong>Otak-otak</strong> — spiced fish paste wrapped in banana leaf and grilled over charcoal — is sold by roadside vendors for RM1–2 per piece. Buy five and eat them walking. <strong>Kuih</strong> (bite-sized Malay cakes and sweets) appear at morning markets and cost RM0.50–1 each. Try <strong>kuih lapis</strong> (rainbow layered cake) and <strong>ondeh-ondeh</strong> (pandan balls filled with palm sugar).</p>\n\n<p>For drinks, the <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled tea) is an art form. Watch the vendor pour boiling tea between two cups from arm's length, creating a frothy, perfectly mixed drink. <strong>Cham</strong> (coffee mixed with tea) is another local favourite. Fresh sugarcane juice costs RM3 and is the best remedy for JB's tropical heat.</p>\n\n<h2>Budget Breakdown</h2>\n<p>Here's what a day of eating in JB actually costs:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Roti canai + teh tarik — RM5</li>\n  <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Hawker centre mixed rice — RM8</li>\n  <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Otak-otak + sugarcane juice — RM5</li>\n  <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Night market feast — RM20</li>\n  <li><strong>Total:</strong> RM38 per day (approximately SGD11 or USD8)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>That's three full meals, snacks, and drinks for less than the cost of one restaurant meal in Singapore. This is why backpackers who discover JB's food scene never want to leave.</p>\n\n<p>Ready to eat your way through JB? <a href=\"/book\">Book a capsule at Pelangi</a> and start your food adventure in one of the best eating neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"singapore-to-jb-day-trip-causeway-guide\",\n    title: \"Singapore to JB Day Trip Guide: How to Cross the Causeway (2026)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"The complete step-by-step guide to crossing the Causeway from Singapore to Johor Bahru — transport options, immigration tips, costs, and what to do once you arrive.\",\n    date: \"2026-02-25\",\n    category: \"Travel Tips\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"causeway\", \"border-crossing\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/causeway-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Why Cross the Causeway?</h2>\n<p>The Johor–Singapore Causeway is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world, connecting Singapore's Woodlands district to Johor Bahru, Malaysia. For travellers based in Singapore, JB offers dramatically cheaper food, shopping, and accommodation — making it the most popular day trip from the island. For backpackers, it's even more strategic: stay in JB at a fraction of Singapore prices and day-trip into Singapore whenever you want.</p>\n\n<p>The crossing itself is straightforward once you know the drill. This guide covers every option, common mistakes, and what to expect in 2026.</p>\n\n<h2>Option 1: Bus from Woodlands (Most Popular)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Causeway Link CW1</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit 170</strong> buses depart from Woodlands Checkpoint on the Singapore side. Here's the process:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Get to Woodlands:</strong> Take the MRT to Woodlands or Kranji station. From Kranji, bus 160 goes directly to the checkpoint.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clear Singapore immigration:</strong> Everyone exits the bus, walks through Singapore passport control, and re-boards.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross the Causeway:</strong> The bus drives across the 1km bridge. Takes 5 minutes without traffic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clear Malaysian immigration:</strong> Everyone exits again, clears Malaysian passport control at JB CIQ (Customs, Immigration & Quarantine).</li>\n  <li><strong>Arrive in JB:</strong> You're now at JB Sentral, connected to City Square Mall and the city centre.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cost: SGD1.50–2 per way. The entire crossing takes 30–60 minutes on a good day, or 2–3 hours during peak periods (Friday evenings, public holidays, school holidays).</p>\n\n<h2>Option 2: Train (RTS Link — Coming Soon)</h2>\n<p>The Johor Bahru–Singapore Rapid Transit System (RTS Link) has been in development and will eventually connect Bukit Chagar in JB to Woodlands North in Singapore. Until it's operational, the bus remains your best bet. Check KTMB for any shuttle train services that may be running between JB Sentral and Woodlands.</p>\n\n<h2>Option 3: Private Car or Grab</h2>\n<p>You can take a Grab from Singapore to JB, but you'll need to exit the car at both immigration checkpoints and re-board. Some drivers won't do cross-border trips. A more reliable option is booking a private transfer — expect SGD40–60 for the car. For solo backpackers, the bus is significantly cheaper and barely slower.</p>\n\n<h2>Immigration Tips</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Passport validity:</strong> Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months from the date of entry into Malaysia.</li>\n  <li><strong>Malaysia Digital Arrival Card (MDAC):</strong> Since 2024, most nationalities must complete the MDAC online before arrival. Do this before you reach the border — it takes 5 minutes and is free.</li>\n  <li><strong>Peak hours to avoid:</strong> Friday 5–9pm (Singapore to JB), Sunday 5–9pm (JB to Singapore). Weekday mornings are the smoothest.</li>\n  <li><strong>Keep your departure card:</strong> Malaysian immigration staples a small card into your passport. Don't lose it — you need it when leaving Malaysia.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What to Do Once You Arrive in JB</h2>\n<p>JB CIQ drops you at <strong>JB Sentral</strong>, the city's main transport hub. From here:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>City Square Mall</strong> is connected directly — air-conditioned shopping, food court, and money changers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Jalan Tan Hiok Nee</strong> (10 min walk) — heritage street with indie cafes, art galleries, and the best street murals in JB.</li>\n  <li><strong>Taman Pelangi</strong> (10 min Grab ride, RM8) — the neighbourhood where <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> is located, surrounded by hawker centres and local restaurants.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>For a complete rundown of activities, attractions, and local tips, check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Backpacker's Guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Costs: Singapore vs JB</h2>\n<p>The whole reason to cross the Causeway is the price difference:</p>","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1100}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RAnEdQ2xwMkbZHG9qnwcMc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I understand the structure. Now let me see what comes after in the file to find another complete blog post example to understand the full pattern, especially to see where the posts array ends.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4393,"cache_read_input_tokens":72285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4393},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6da17fb6-3289-4255-a288-226ac7439267","uuid":"f7befd88-dbad-470a-980e-7951b8eb21dc"}
